# keur portability control: emplace_back constructing a constructor-less
# aggregate struct. This compiles on libstdc++ (Linux) and MSYS2 (Windows) but
# FAILS on macOS libc++ (Xcode): C++20 std::construct_at requires a real
# constructor, so `vec.emplace_back(a, b)` on an aggregate `struct{...};` errors
# with "no matching function for call to 'construct_at'". The whole keur macOS
# CI build broke on exactly this across 7 structs (RuleCategoryCount,
# HookInfoItem, Layer, ScannerConfigFile, HookManager, RuleFinding, Row) — a
# multi-round whack-a-mole that a single rule would have caught at commit time.
#
# The fix is always the same: use push_back({...}) (brace/aggregate init) instead
# of emplace_back(...). A regex cannot know whether the element type has a
# constructor, so this WARNs (not blocks) on the risky shapes: emplace_back with
# multiple arguments, or a single &address / brace — the forms that hit an
# aggregate. emplace_back of a single string/number (std::string, counters) is
# left quiet. Review each hit: if the target is an aggregate, switch to
# push_back({...}). @see docs/adr/ADR-005 (platform), ADR-006 (portability).
id: KEUR-PORT-007
title: emplace_back on a (possibly) constructor-less aggregate — breaks on macOS libc++
category: architecture
severity: warning
engine: pattern
action: warn
target:
  extensions: .cpp .cc .h .hpp
  exclude_paths: vendor/ _test.cpp test/ tests/ build/ dist/
patterns:
  # multi-argument emplace_back: an aggregate {a, b, ...} built via parens.
  - regex: \.emplace_back\(\s*[^)]*,[^)]*\)
    message: "emplace_back(a, b, ...) constructs the element in place and needs a real constructor; on a constructor-less aggregate struct this fails on macOS libc++ (construct_at). Use push_back({a, b, ...}) if the element type is an aggregate. @see KEUR-PORT-007"
  # single &address emplace_back: aggregate wrapping a pointer, e.g. Row{&n}.
  - regex: \.emplace_back\(\s*&\w
    message: "emplace_back(&x) on an aggregate that wraps a pointer fails on macOS libc++ (construct_at needs a constructor). Use push_back({&x}). @see KEUR-PORT-007"
confidence: 0.55
fix: "Replace emplace_back(args) with push_back({args}) whenever the vector's element type is a constructor-less aggregate struct. Brace-init works on libstdc++, libc++ and MSVC; emplace_back's in-place construction does not, because C++20 std::construct_at requires a matching constructor. See ADR-005/ADR-006."
